WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.240
So far, your workflow runs only when you
run it inside the editor. Deploying

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.800
changes that. It gives your workflow a
real address so the outside world can

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:15.580
reach it. As an API, other systems can
call, a chat people can talk to, or a tool

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:20.760
other AI agents can use. The same
workflow, now live and running on demand.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:27.040
Let's show how to ship one. This is your
working workflow. You type, I want a

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.800
refund. It lands on the start block, the
agent classifies it, and the response

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.900
comes back, billing. But it only runs when
you run it right here in the editor.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:40.580
Deploying publishes that workflow and
gives it an address. Nothing inside

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:45.480
changes, the same blocks, the same start.
Now an external request can reach that

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:51.320
address and flow straight into the start
block, and the workflow runs. A request

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:57.540
comes in from somewhere else, a refund
from customer 4821. It lands on the same

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:02.060
input row. The agent runs, the answer
comes back billing, and the reply goes

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:07.820
back out. An external call is just a run.
The only difference is where it came from.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.500
You can also deploy the same workflow as a
chat. Deploying as a chat gives it a chat

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.840
page, and anyone who opens it gets a
conversation. Each message they send flows

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.320
into the same start block as its input.
The agent thinks, and the reply that comes

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:28.640
back is whatever output you selected.
Here, billing. Underneath the

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:35.100
conversation, it's the same ordinary run.
Every time. That's a deployment. The

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:40.020
same workflow you built, now running as an
operational system. A call arrives, the

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:44.040
chain settles in order, and the reply goes
back out whenever they call it, with no

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:45.040
one in the editor.
